{"duration_ms":0,"id":18,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.11","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"Fixed in `client.py:5`. `_key` returned just `path`, so params never entered the cache key.\n\n```python\ndef _key(path, params):\n    # Sort by name so that {\"x\": 1, \"y\": 2} and {\"y\": 2, \"x\": 1} share an entry.\n    return (path, tuple(sorted(params.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[0])))\n```\n\nTwo details worth noting:\n\n- **Sorted, not `repr`/insertion order** — `{\"x\": 1, \"y\": 2}` and `{\"y\": 2, \"x\": 1}` are the same request and must share one entry. `test_param_order_does_not_create_a_second_entry` exists specifically to catch a fix that keys on stringified params, so it's a real constraint, not incidental.\n- **Sort by key only** (`key=lambda kv: kv[0]`) rather than sorting whole `(k, v)` pairs. Tuple sorting falls back to comparing values when two names tie, which can't happen in a dict — but sorting pairs also raises `TypeError` on mixed-type values in some paths. Sorting on the name sidesteps that entirely.\n\nVerified: `{'x': 1}` and `{'x': 2}` now return distinct bodies and record 2 real fetches; all 3 tests pass.\n\nOne limitation I did not change: keys must be hashable, so a param value like a list (`{\"ids\": [1, 2]}`) will raise `TypeError` at the `key in _CACHE` lookup. That was equally true before in the sense that such values were simply ignored, and no test or caller covers it — say the word if list-valued params are in scope and I'll normalize them.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011CevGhBffHvZ7mGMZG5xM7","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":229,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":229,"cache_read_input_tokens":18373,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"}},"start_ms":0,"usage":{"cache_read":18373,"cache_write_1h":229,"input":2,"output":7}}
